          "citation": "2023-NMSC-020, 536 P.3d 482",
          "court": "New Mexico Supreme Court",
          "year": 2023,
          "date_decided": "2023-07-24",
          "holding": "The Court held that the plain language of the Family Violence Protection Act does not require a petitioner to show imminent danger or injury to obtain an order of protection, and it affirmed the Court of Appeals' reversal of the denial of an order to a petitioner who alleged she was sexually assaulted as a child.",
          "relevance": "It is the New Mexico Supreme Court's controlling construction of the Family Violence Protection Act, the statute under which a protective order is obtained to keep an abuser away without removing the child, and it removes the imminence barrier that had been imposed on child sexual-abuse petitioners.",
          "quote": "We hold that the plain language of the FVPA does not require petitioners to provide a showing of imminent danger or injury in seeking an order of protection",
